        {
          "text": "**Short answer:** the `next_leg` link is empty \u2014 the tool reports `chain_break_reason: impossible_turn` \u2014 but the aircraft did fly again out of RSW. The rotation link on tail **N247WN** shows a fourth leg that day, **2026-01-07|WN|120|RSW|MKE|1505**.\n\n**What the tail did on 2026-01-07** (N247WN, four legs):\n\n| Leg | Sched dep UTC | Dep delay | Arr delay |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026-01-07\\|WN\\|1165\\|MCO\\|BUF\\|0900 | 14:00 | \u22123 | +23 (all NAS) |\n| 2026-01-07\\|WN\\|1165\\|BUF\\|BWI\\|1220 | 17:20 | +28 | +4 |\n| 2026-01-07\\|WN\\|1016\\|BWI\\|RSW\\|1430 | 19:30 | +10 | \u221215 |\n| 2026-01-07\\|WN\\|120\\|RSW\\|MKE\\|1505 | 20:05 | +205 | +205 (91 carrier, 114 late aircraft) |\n\n**Why the link breaks.** It is a scheduling impossibility, not a missing aircraft. WN1016 is scheduled into RSW at 22:20 UTC (17:20 local), while WN120 is scheduled out of RSW at 20:05 UTC (15:05 local) \u2014 the outbound is booked to leave 135 minutes *before* its own inbound is due to land. That is a negative scheduled turn, so `next_leg` refuses to connect them even though the same tail flies both.\n\nIn practice the turn happened, late: WN1016 landed 15 minutes early (actual 22:05 UTC) and WN120 pushed 205 minutes late (actual 23:30 UTC), giving about 85 minutes on the ground at RSW. WN120's own cause split confirms the dependence \u2014 114 of the 205 minutes are coded late_aircraft, with 91 as carrier.\n\n**Caveat:** the 20:05 UTC scheduled departure against a 22:20 UTC scheduled arrival cannot both be correct for one airframe, so either the published schedule for WN120 or the tail assignment in this extract is off. Nothing in this data \u2014 no aircraft type, no crew, no tail-swap record \u2014 tells me which, and resolving it would need the original schedule/tail-assignment source.",
